WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.880
In the near Chandrasekhar model, an accreting carbon

00:00:02.880 --> 00:00:05.440
and oxygen white dwarf contracts and heats until

00:00:05.440 --> 00:00:09.340
carbon burning runs away. Degeneracy pressure responds weakly

00:00:09.340 --> 00:00:12.480
to temperature, so expansion cooling initially fails to

00:00:12.480 --> 00:00:15.940
stabilize the burning. This is a thermonuclear disruption,

00:00:16.080 --> 00:00:18.620
rather than an iron core collapsing into a

00:00:18.620 --> 00:00:21.680
neutron star. Other Type One A progenitor channels

00:00:21.680 --> 00:00:22.500
also exist.

00:00:23.230 --> 00:00:27.450
The explosion makes radioactive nickel fifty six. It

00:00:27.450 --> 00:00:30.870
decays into cobalt fifty six, then stable iron

00:00:30.870 --> 00:00:34.290
fifty six. Deposited energy from that chain heats

00:00:34.290 --> 00:00:36.770
the expanding debris and sustains the light. The

00:00:36.770 --> 00:00:39.310
later radioactive heating differs from the rapid burning

00:00:39.310 --> 00:00:41.710
that made the unstable nuclei in the first

00:00:41.710 --> 00:00:42.150
place.

00:00:42.880 --> 00:00:46.080
Calibrate the event’s luminosity before comparing it with

00:00:46.080 --> 00:00:50.060
received brightness. For equal calibrated luminosities and negligible

00:00:50.060 --> 00:00:52.800
extinction, an event sixteen times fainter is four

00:00:52.800 --> 00:00:55.560
times farther away. Dust can also make it

00:00:55.560 --> 00:01:00.160
fainter; ignoring that dimming overestimates distance. Standardizable candles

00:01:00.160 --> 00:01:03.060
need calibration, not an assumption that every explosion

00:01:03.060 --> 00:01:04.220
is perfectly identical.
